.TH PAIN 1 "2026-07-30" "pain 1.11.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pain \- cross-platform, multi-pane terminal emulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pain
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pain
is a terminal emulator for Windows, macOS, and Linux with nested split
panes, pane grouping with broadcast input, and session persistence. It is
built on Alacritty's VT and PTY backend with an original GPU-rendered
frontend.
.PP
It takes no positional arguments and cannot be told what to run: every pane
starts the configured default shell. See
.B default_shell
under
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary, including the resolved config file path for this
machine, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-era = \fINAME\fR
Use a retro era for this session only, without writing to the config file.
.B \-\-era=list
prints the available eras and exits. See
.B RETRO ERAS
below.
.TP
.B \-\-settings
Open the settings window at startup, rather than reaching it through a
pane's context menu. The settings window is a separate operating-system
window with its own surface and rendering context, so this exists to make
reproducing a report about it a single command.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose [ =\fILIST\fR ]
Enable diagnostic logging on standard error.
.I LIST
is a comma-separated list of categories. The bare flag enables
.B general
alone, because the other categories are high-frequency enough to drown it
out; each is an explicit opt-in.
.RS
.TP
.B general
Startup information, config load and reload, shell spawn and exit.
.TP
.B mouse
Every mouse event: motion, clicks, drags, wheel.
.TP
.B pty
Every chunk read from, and every keystroke written to, a shell.
.TP
.B foreground
The per-pane foreground-process scan that keeps pane titles current, which
runs roughly every 500ms per pane.
.TP
.B all
All of the above.
.RE
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
Every shortcut below is a default and can be changed; see
.B [keybindings]
under
.BR CONFIGURATION .
Keys not listed here are passed through to the shell unmodified.
.SS Panes
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+O
Split the focused pane horizontally.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+E
Split the focused pane vertically.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+W
Close the focused pane. Closing the last one exits.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+X
Zoom the focused pane to fill the window, or restore it.
.TP
.BR Alt+Up ", " Alt+Down ", " Alt+Left ", " Alt+Right
Move focus to the neighbouring pane in that direction.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Shift+Up ", " Ctrl+Shift+Down ", " Ctrl+Shift+Left ", " Ctrl+Shift+Right
Resize the focused pane in that direction.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+Q
Quit, saving the session.
.SS Clipboard
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
On Windows and Linux: copy the selection if there is one, and otherwise
send an interrupt to the running program, as it always has. Copying clears
the selection, so a second press interrupts rather than copying again.
On macOS this is left alone and always interrupts.
.TP
.B Ctrl+V
On Windows and Linux: paste. This displaces readline's
.B quoted\-insert
binding; set
.RB \(dq ctrl\ v \(dq
to
.RB \(dq none \(dq
in
.B [keybindings]
to get it back. On macOS this is left alone.
.TP
.BR Cmd+C ", " Cmd+V
macOS only: copy and paste.
.TP
.BR Cmd+Q ", " Cmd+W
macOS only: quit, and close the focused pane.
.PP
.BR Ctrl+Shift+C " and " Ctrl+Shift+V,
the usual Linux-terminal clipboard chords, are not bound. They existed only
because the unshifted pair was unavailable, which is no longer true on
either platform. Bind them back in
.B [keybindings]
if you have the muscle memory:
.RB \(dq ctrl\ shift\ c \(dq
to
.RB \(dq copy \(dq,
and
.RB \(dq ctrl\ shift\ v \(dq
to
.RB \(dq paste \(dq.
.PP
Pasted text is wrapped in bracketed-paste markers when the running program
has asked for them, so a shell holds it at the prompt for review rather
than executing each line as it arrives. When the program has not asked for
them, a multi-line paste asks for confirmation first and shows exactly what
will be sent; see
.B confirm_multiline_paste
under
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.SS Broadcast
Broadcast sends your keystrokes to more than one pane at once. It has no
default chord and is set from the title-bar context menu, but the actions
.BR broadcast_off ", " broadcast_group ", and " broadcast_all
can be bound in
.BR [keybindings] .
Assigning a pane to a group is menu-only, because it needs a group name
that a chord cannot carry.
.SH MOUSE
.TP
.B Double-click
Select a word.
.TP
.B Triple-click
Select a line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+click
Open the link under the pointer in your browser. Holding
.B Ctrl
underlines it first, so you can see what will open before clicking.
A link a program marked explicitly with the
.B OSC 8
escape sequence takes precedence, and otherwise the visible text is matched
for a URL. Only
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
.BR ftp ,
.B ssh
and
.B mailto
are ever handed to the operating system, whichever way the link was found:
a terminal prints paths and arbitrary program output constantly, and
opening any of that with the system's default handler on a click is a
wider capability than this is willing to grant.
.TP
.B Shift+click
Force a local text selection, bypassing mouse reporting. This is the
standard escape hatch for selecting text inside full-screen programs such
as vim or htop, which would otherwise consume the click themselves.
.TP
.B Right-click on a pane's title bar
Open the pane menu: split, arrange, group, broadcast, swap shell, settings.
.TP
.B Right-click on a pane's contents
Open the terminal menu: copy, paste, close.
.TP
.B Scroll wheel
Scroll back through that pane's history.
.SH PANE ACTIVITY
A pane you are not currently focused on shows a dot in its title bar when
something has happened in it, so a build or a log tail off to the side does
not have to be watched.
.TP
.B Blue
The shell produced output since that pane was last focused. Cleared by
focusing the pane.
.TP
.B Red
A program rang the terminal bell. Cleared by typing into the pane.
.PP
A bell outranks output, since it is a program explicitly asking for
attention rather than something a pane merely did, and the two are cleared
differently on purpose. Output only matters for a pane you are not
watching, so looking at it suffices. A bell usually rings the instant a
command starts \(em while its own pane is still focused, because you just
pressed Enter there \(em so it survives focus and clears on the next input
to that pane, which is what actually demonstrates you saw it.
.SH RETRO ERAS
An era is a period look for a specific machine rather than a general retro
mood: a palette, screen effects, and a typeface, bundled under one name.
.PP
The \fBmatrix\fR era is the one entry on this list that never existed.
.TP
.B green
IBM 5151, 1981 \(em P1 green phosphor.
.TP
.B amber
Amber CRT, mid-1980s \(em P3 phosphor, easier on the eyes.
.TP
.B cga
IBM 5153, 1981 \(em the CGA 16.
.TP
.B bbs
Dial-up BBS, ~1993 \(em the CGA palette ANSI art was drawn for.
.TP
.B c64
Commodore 64, 1982 \(em VIC-II blues.
.TP
.B matrix
Not a machine, and not 1999's idea of one either.
.PP
Set one in
.BR [retro] ,
choose it in Settings under Retro, or try one for a single session without
touching the config file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pain \-\-era=amber
pain \-\-era=list
.fi
.RE
.PP
An era can also be set live by a shell or a script, using this terminal's own
escape sequence:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf '\\e]7331;era=green\\a'
.fi
.RE
.PP
That is session-only and never written to the config file, so a restart
returns to whatever was chosen there. The payload is an era
.I name
rather than a set of colors, so program output cannot produce an unreadable
screen \(em the worst it can do is make the terminal look like 1985.
.PP
Anything set explicitly wins over the era, including
.BR appearance.background_color :
a value someone chose is not overridden by trying a costume on.
.PP
The screen effects are deliberately static \(em no animation and no time
input. They add one draw to a frame that was already being rendered, so an
idle terminal still renders nothing and still sleeps.
.PP
Eras name period typefaces rather than bundling them, and use one if it is
installed \(em \fBVT323\fR for the phosphor eras, \fBPx437 IBM VGA 8x16\fR
for the DOS ones, \fBC64 Pro Mono\fR for \fBc64\fR. None ship with this
program; see the README for where to obtain them. Without one an era still
applies its palette and effects and keeps the configured font.
.PP
Effects cover the terminal grid only. Pane title bars are chrome and are left
alone.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is a TOML file, read at startup and re-read when it changes.
Every key is optional and anything missing uses its default, so a partial
file is valid. The file does not exist until settings are saved from the
settings window or you create it yourself.
.PP
A malformed file is never fatal: a parse failure at startup falls back to
defaults with a message on standard error, and a bad edit while running
keeps the settings already loaded rather than resetting them. Individual
bad
.B [keybindings]
lines are skipped one at a time, not treated as poisoning the whole table.
Numeric settings are clamped to the ranges documented below, with a note on
standard error saying what changed \(em an out-of-range value is used at the
nearest end of its range, and a value that is not a number at all falls back
to its default.
.SS [general]
.TP
.B default_shell
String. The shell every new pane starts. Empty (the default) means the
platform's own default:
.B $SHELL
on Unix, and the configured default shell on Windows.
.TP
.B scrollback_lines
Integer, default
.BR 5000 ,
maximum
.BR 1000000 .
Lines of history kept per pane. Changing it applies to panes that are
already open, not just new ones.
.TP
.B confirm_multiline_paste
Boolean, default
.BR true .
Ask before pasting text that would run more than one command into a program
that has not enabled bracketed paste. Turning this off removes the last
check on an unreviewed paste executing arbitrary commands the instant it
arrives.
.SS [appearance]
.TP
.B theme
String, default
.BR \(dqGraphite\(dq .
Names one of over 600 built-in color themes, which supply the 16 ANSI
colors programs draw with plus the default foreground and background.
Familiar names apply:
.BR Dracula ,
.BR \(dqTokyo Night\(dq ,
.BR \(dqGruvbox Dark\(dq ,
.BR \(dqSolarized Light\(dq .
Matching ignores case, and an unrecognised name falls back to the default
rather than failing to load. The settings window lists them with a filter.
.TP
.B ligatures
Boolean, default
.BR false .
Shape each row's text in runs so a font can render
.B !=
as a single glyph. Requires a font that provides ligatures \(em Fira Code,
JetBrains Mono, Cascadia Code, Iosevka \(em and has no visible effect with
any other. Off by default because glyph positions then come from the font's
own advances rather than the cell grid, so a font whose ligature widths do
not match its cell width drifts out of alignment, and because shaping costs
per-frame work the default path does not. A ligature is never applied
across a color change or across the cursor.
.TP
.B font_family
String, default
.BR \(dqmonospace\(dq .
Any installed monospaced family. The settings window lists what is
available.
.TP
.B font_size
Whole number from
.B 6
to
.BR 48 ,
default
.BR 13 .
Scaled by the display's DPI factor, so this is a logical size and matches
other applications on a scaled display rather than being raw pixels.
.TP
.B transparency
Whole-number percentage from
.B 0
(fully transparent) to
.B 100
(opaque), default
.BR 100 .
A configuration file written before version 1.9 stored this as a
.BR 0.0 - 1.0
fraction; those are still read and converted.
.TP
.B background_color
String,
.B #rrggbb
hex, default empty.
Overrides the terminal background. Empty \(em the default \(em means the
background follows whichever
.B theme
is chosen, which is usually what you want, since a theme's background is
part of its design and a light theme forced onto a dark ground is
unreadable. A non-empty value wins over the theme. An unparseable value
falls back to the theme's background rather than failing to load.
.TP
.B accent_color
String,
.B #rrggbb
hex, default
.BR \(dq#7fa2d6\(dq .
Used for the cursor, the selection highlight, and interactive highlights in
menus and the settings panel. Semantic colors, such as the broadcast-target
border, are fixed and not affected by this.
.SS [retro]
Period looks, off by default. See
.B RETRO ERAS
below.
.TP
.B era
String, default empty.
Names an era. Empty means off, which renders byte-for-byte as if this section
did not exist. An unrecognised name is treated as off rather than failing the
load.
.IP
An active era overrides
.B appearance.theme
with its own palette, but never writes to it \(em turning the era off
restores the chosen theme, so trying one on cannot lose a setting.
.TP
.B scanlines
Integer 0-100, default absent. Strength of the horizontal scanline pattern.
Absent follows the era;
.B 0
turns it off while keeping the rest of the era.
.TP
.B hum
Integer 0-100, default absent. Strength of the drifting mains-hum bar \(em the
soft band that crept up an old monitor when its power supply and the mains
supply were slightly out of step. Absent follows the era.
.IP
This is the only effect that moves, and so the only one that keeps the
terminal drawing while it sits idle. The cost is bounded: it stops entirely
when the window loses focus, and redraws well below the display's refresh rate,
since the bar takes about nine seconds to cross the screen. Set it to
.B 0
to recover the idle cost completely.
.TP
.B vignette
Integer 0-100, default absent. Strength of the curved-glass effect. Absent
follows the era.
.IP
This both darkens toward the corners and lifts the centre with a faint glow in
the theme's foreground color. The lift is what makes the darkening visible at
all: every CRT palette has a near-black background, and darkening near-black
by a quarter moves it three levels out of 255.
.SS [cursor]
.TP
.B style
One of
.BR block " (default), " underline ", " beam .
.SS [keybindings]
A table of chord to action name, overriding the built-in defaults. A chord
is
.BR + -separated,
case-insensitive, with modifiers in any order and exactly one non-modifier
segment: a single character, or
.BR up ", " down ", " left ", or " right .
The modifier
.B ctrl
may also be written
.BR control ,
and
.B cmd
may be written
.BR logo ", " super ", or " win .
.PP
The action name
.B none
unbinds a chord with no replacement, which is how you hand a chord back to
the shell. Recognized actions are:
.PP
.RS
.BR split_horizontal ", " split_vertical ", " close_pane ", " quit ,
.BR focus_up ", " focus_down ", " focus_left ", " focus_right ,
.BR resize_up ", " resize_down ", " resize_left ", " resize_right ,
.BR toggle_zoom ", " copy ", " copy_or_interrupt ", " paste ,
.BR broadcast_off ", " broadcast_group ", " broadcast_all .
.RE
.PP
Overrides are applied on top of a fresh copy of the defaults every time the
file is read, so deleting a line restores that chord's built-in binding
rather than leaving it stuck at the old override.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[keybindings]
"ctrl shift t" = "split_vertical"
"ctrl v" = "none"
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pain/config.toml
Configuration on Linux, honouring
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
if set.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/pain/config.toml
Configuration on macOS.
.TP
.I %APPDATA%\[rs]pain\[rs]config.toml
Configuration on Windows.
.PP
The session file, holding the saved layout, window size, and each pane's
working directory, shell, and group, sits alongside the config file in the
same directory.
On Windows a pane's working directory usually cannot be determined, so
restored panes open in the home directory; the layout, window size, shell
and group are restored as normal. Run
.B pain \-\-help
to see the resolved path on the machine you are on.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
On Unix, the default shell for new panes when
.B default_shell
is unset.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Filter for internal log output, defaulting to
.BR warn .
This controls messages from the graphics stack and other libraries; it is
separate from
.BR \-\-verbose ,
which controls this application's own diagnostics.
.PP
Panes are started with
.B TERM=xterm\-256color
and
.BR COLORTERM=truecolor .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B pain
exits 0 when the last pane is closed or you quit, and non-zero if it could
not start, most often because no GPU adapter or display was available.
